# XSH pthread_barrier_init(), pthread_barrier_destroy()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# How a barrier blocks and releases is tested in
# xsh/pthread_barrier_wait. Destroying or re-initializing a barrier that
# threads are blocked on, and using a barrier that was not initialized,
# are undefined and not tested. Waits run in helper threads, with 10 s
# limits.

assertion  XSH.pthread_barrier_init.001
anchor     functions/pthread_barrier_init.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_barrier_init.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       pthread_barrier_init() with a null attributes pointer and count
           2 returns 0, two threads then pass the barrier (one getting
           PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD, one 0), and
           pthread_barrier_destroy() returns 0.
test       t/001-init-null-attr.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_barrier_init.002
anchor     functions/pthread_barrier_init.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       A destroyed barrier can be initialized again, with a different
           count: after destroy of a barrier for 2 threads,
           pthread_barrier_init() with count 3 returns 0, and three
           threads then pass it (exactly one getting
           PTHREAD_BARRIER_SERIAL_THREAD).
test       t/002-reinit.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_barrier_init.003
anchor     functions/pthread_barrier_init.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2008
says       pthread_barrier_init() with a count of 0 returns EINVAL.
test       t/003-count-zero.c
